#!/usr/bin/env bash
# backup: add the plugin's own data to a dot-backup snapshot. (optional)
#
# Run by dot-backup for every enabled plugin whose `installed` hook succeeds. Copy or generate
# whatever is worth keeping into $DOT_BACKUP_DEST; it is stored in the snapshot next to your
# dotfiles, under .plugin-data/NAME/, and is yours to lay out. Your dotfiles themselves
# (including this plugin's folder) are already in the snapshot, so keep only what lives
# elsewhere or is generated: editor settings outside the whitelist, the output of
# `code --list-extensions`, `pip freeze`, a pinned SDK version, ...
#
# Contract
#   Exit 0      success. Writing nothing is fine (no folder is kept for the plugin).
#   Exit != 0   failure: reported, and the backup carries on without this plugin's data.
#   Never copy credentials or keys. After the hook, dot-backup scans what you stored with the
#   same checks as dot-doctor (key and credential file names, private key blocks, common
#   token formats). If anything matches, your data is deleted and the plugin is reported as
#   failed, so a backup never quietly keeps a secret.
#   Same rules as `init`: non-interactive (stdin closed), output shown only on failure, no
#   time limit, and a `needs-sudo` file in the plugin folder gets the sudo password asked for
#   before this hook starts.
#
# Environment
#   DOT_PLUGIN, DOT_PLUGIN_DIR, DOT_OS, DOT_ROOT, DOT_COMMON: as in `init`.
#   DOT_HOOK         "backup"
#   DOT_BACKUP_DEST  existing, empty folder reserved for this plugin in the snapshot
#
# Arguments: none.
#
# Also runs for the "pre-restore" snapshot dot-restore takes before overwriting anything, so
# it must be safe to run any time. Example:
#   code --list-extensions > "$DOT_BACKUP_DEST/extensions.txt"
